[
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-05",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-05",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Overview and justification",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>This course assumes you can already reference accurately and consistently, and that you already understand why academic work does so. What it adds is judgement under pressure: the ethical choices that referencing and attribution raise once you are working independently, in shared work, or in a professional role where getting it wrong has consequences beyond a lost mark.</p><p>By the end you will be able to justify your own course of action when a weak result tempts you toward a stronger impression than the evidence supports, act to correct an unsupported claim in shared work, respond to a fairness issue around an unacknowledged contribution, critique your own habits against a profession's ethical expectations, and recognise and act on a new ethical responsibility without being asked.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-10",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-10",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Learning outcomes",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>By the end of this course, you will be able to:</p><ul><li>Justify a course of action when a weak or disappointing result raises an ethical choice between honest reporting and presenting a stronger impression.</li><li>Justify acting to correct an unsupported figure or claim in shared work, upholding honesty even where the error would otherwise go unnoticed.</li><li>Justify a response to a fairness issue in shared work, weighing the discomfort of speaking up against the responsibility that courage requires.</li><li>Raise an unacknowledged contribution with those involved, treating them with respect, and help secure a fairer outcome even where the conversation is uncomfortable.</li><li>Critique your own habits of honesty and attribution against the ethical expectations of a profession, identifying where those expectations will tighten.</li><li>Recognise and act on an ethical responsibility in a new professional role without being asked, and explain the habit to someone newer than yourself.</li></ul>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-15",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-15",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "slot__wrapper-open__practice-connection",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "In real academic practice",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>These are not abstract ethical puzzles: they are the situations that come up in a dissertation, a group project, a placement or a first professional role, where a weak result, an unattributed contribution, or an unfamiliar source type lands on your desk with no one checking your working. The judgement this course builds sits alongside your critical and research practice, informing the same honesty that underpins every claim you make and every source you cite elsewhere.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-20",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-20",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Signpost",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The course has six short lessons, each built around a situation you might actually face. First, a weak result and the choice between honest reporting and a stronger impression. Second, correcting an unsupported claim in shared work. Third, a fairness issue in shared work. Fourth, raising an unacknowledged contribution. Fifth, weighing your own habits against a profession's expectations. Sixth, acting on a new ethical responsibility without being asked. Work through them in order; each builds on the one before.</p>",
    "instruction": "Work through the six lessons in order.",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-25",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-25",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "Course instructions",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p><strong>Start and finish with a confidence check.</strong> Before the lessons, you'll rate how confident you feel about each part of this topic. You'll rate the same statements again at the end, so you can see what has changed. Your ratings are yours: they aren't marked and no one else sees them.</p><p><strong>The activities in the lessons are for practice.</strong> Questions, examples and reflective prompts are there to help you think, and they don't count towards passing. Reflective prompts are private and optional.</p><p><strong>The test at the end is the one that counts.</strong> To complete the course you need to take the final test and score at least 70%. You can retake it.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
    "_id": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-c-30",
    "_parentId": "referencing-v2-advanced-W-OPEN-b-30",
    "_type": "component",
    "_component": "text",
    "_classes": "",
    "_layout": "full",
    "title": "How this course works",
    "displayTitle": "",
    "body": "<p>The examples and activities in this course use invented people, authors and sources to show how things work. Unless we say otherwise, any name, citation or reference you meet in an example is fictional.</p><p>Where a lesson draws on real work, you'll find it listed under <strong>Lesson Sources</strong> at the bottom of that page. Every real source used in the course is gathered under <strong>Course Sources</strong> at the end.</p>",
    "instruction": "",
    "_pageLevelProgress": {
      "_isEnabled": true,
      "_isCompletionIndicatorEnabled": false
    }
  },
  {
